data: {"repo":{"owner":"lukeed","name":"clsx","url":"https://github.com/lukeed/clsx","description":"A tiny (239B) utility for constructing `className` strings conditionally.","isPrivate":false,"stars":9812,"forks":185,"openIssues":17,"primaryLanguage":"JavaScript","pushedAt":"2024-06-10T15:48:22Z","defaultBranch":"master","sizeKb":139,"license":"MIT","topics":[],"headSha":"925494cf31bcd97d3337aacd34e659e80cae7fe2"},"overallScore":26,"level":{"id":"L2","name":"Assisted","band":[25,44],"tagline":"Tools adopted, basic guardrails","description":"AI tools are adopted by many developers and basic guardrails exist (some tests, a linter, CI runs). There are no shared AI conventions, so benefits are individual rather than systemic."},"archetype":"org","adoptionScore":12,"rigorScore":33,"posture":{"id":"early","label":"Getting Started","blurb":"Early on both AI adoption and engineering rigor — pick one axis to advance first."},"aiUsage":{"detected":false,"commitFraction":0,"signals":[]},"contributors":[{"login":"lukeed","name":"Luke Edwards","commits":23,"aiCommits":0,"lastActiveAt":"2024-04-23T05:26:02Z"},{"login":"ViliamKopecky","name":"Viliam Kopecký","commits":1,"aiCommits":0,"lastActiveAt":"2024-04-23T05:24:12Z"},{"login":"jalalazimi","name":"Jalal azimi","commits":1,"aiCommits":0,"lastActiveAt":"2023-12-29T18:09:13Z"},{"login":"acusti","name":"Andrew Patton","commits":1,"aiCommits":0,"lastActiveAt":"2023-10-20T23:29:57Z"},{"login":"andipaetzold","name":"Andi Pätzold","commits":1,"aiCommits":0,"lastActiveAt":"2023-07-16T20:09:15Z"},{"login":"remcohaszing","name":"Remco Haszing","commits":1,"aiCommits":0,"lastActiveAt":"2023-07-15T22:45:51Z"},{"login":"kevinlowe0x3F7","name":"Kevin Lowe","commits":1,"aiCommits":0,"lastActiveAt":"2023-02-28T13:53:17Z"},{"login":"edgarlr","name":"Edgar López","commits":1,"aiCommits":0,"lastActiveAt":"2023-01-18T22:56:20Z"}],"dimensions":[{"id":"D1","name":"AI Tooling & Conventions","weight":0.15,"score":2,"signalScore":0,"llmScore":3,"summary":"No machine-readable AI/agent guidance of any kind was found in the repository — no CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, .cursorrules, copilot-instructions.md, or MCP configuration. Given the codebase's tiny surface area (a single src/index.js and src/lite.js), an agent could probably infer conventions by reading the code, but nothing documents the build pipeline (bin/index.js minifies + gzips into dist/), the test runner (uvu), or constraints like 'never hand-edit dist/'. This is a pure gap rather than a partial one.","evidence":["No machine-readable AI/agent guidance detected (e.g. CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, .cursorrules)"],"strengths":["Codebase is small and uniform enough (two source files, one build script) that conventions are at least discoverable by reading, even without dedicated docs"],"gaps":["No CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md or equivalent documenting build/test/run commands","No documented architecture map (src -> bin build -> dist trio of formats)","No explicit 'never/always' constraints an agent could follow (e.g. don't edit dist/ directly)","No advanced agent tooling: MCP config, subagents, hooks, slash commands, or tool-permission policy"]},{"id":"D2","name":"Automated Testing","weight":0.15,"score":54,"signalScore":53,"llmScore":55,"summary":"Testing is the strongest part of this repository. Three test files (test/index.js, test/lite.js, test/classnames.js) exercise every input permutation — strings, numbers, objects, arrays, deep nesting, and the 'lite' string-only mode — totaling 99 substantive assertions, with a test-to-source ratio of 0.60. A dedicated compatibility suite ported from the `classnames` library validates drop-in behavior. Coverage is tracked via c8 and reported to Codecov in CI. What's missing for a higher score is any advanced rigor layer (mutation testing, contract tests, perf/a11y gating) and an enforced coverage threshold rather than just a badge.","evidence":["Found 3 test files","Coverage tracking configured","High test-to-source ratio (0.60)","Sampled tests assert behavior (99 substantive assertions across 3 sampled test file(s))"],"strengths":["3 focused test files with 99 substantive behavioral assertions covering every documented input type","Dedicated ported compatibility suite (test/classnames.js) guarding against regressions vs. the library it replaces","High test-to-source ratio (0.60) for a library this small","Coverage instrumented via c8 and uploaded to Codecov in CI (badge visible in README)"],"gaps":["No coverage threshold enforced (report-only, not a merge gate)","No mutation testing (Stryker/mutmut) to validate the suite catches injected faults","bench/ directory exists but is manual benchmark.js usage, not a CI-gated performance regression check","No API-schema, contract, or accessibility testing (arguably not applicable to a pure utility, but no equivalent rigor layer exists either)"]},{"id":"D3","name":"CI/CD & Delivery","weight":0.14,"score":52,"signalScore":55,"llmScore":50,"summary":"GitHub Actions CI runs on every push/PR across a wide Node matrix (8–18), builds the bundles, runs the test suite, and reports coverage to Codecov — solid basics for a library this size. However there is no visible release automation (version bumps like '2.1.1' appear to be manual commits followed by presumably manual `npm publish`), no branch protection gating merges, and no delivery-as-code practices (IaC/GitOps/progressive delivery are not applicable to a library, but no auditable release trail exists either).","evidence":["GitHub Actions CI present","CI runs tests","CI runs a build"],"strengths":["GitHub Actions CI on push and pull_request, matrixed across 6 Node versions","CI builds the distributable bundles and runs the full test suite","Coverage collected via c8 and piped to Codecov as part of CI"],"gaps":["No release automation (semantic-release/changesets) — versions appear bumped by hand","No branch protection on master, so CI passing isn't actually required before merge","No documented/automated publish step to npm visible in the workflow","Action refs unpinned (checkout@v3, setup-node@v3, cache@master) rather than pinned by SHA for reproducibility"]},{"id":"D4","name":"Agentic Workflows","weight":0.12,"score":2,"signalScore":0,"llmScore":3,"summary":"No agentic workflows exist anywhere in the pipeline: no AI review bots (CodeRabbit, Claude/Copilot review), no LLM steps inside CI, no auto-fix or auto-PR tooling, and not even non-AI dependency automation (Dependabot/Renovate) is configured. All review and merge decisions in this repository are entirely human-driven and manual.","evidence":["No agentic/AI-in-CI workflows detected (e.g. AI review bots, LLM steps in CI, auto-merge)"],"strengths":[],"gaps":["No AI code-review bot wired into pull requests","No LLM invocation steps inside the CI workflow","No auto-fix/auto-format bot or auto-PR automation","No dependency-update automation at all (not even a non-AI baseline like Dependabot)"]},{"id":"D5","name":"Documentation & Knowledge","weight":0.09,"score":30,"signalScore":30,"llmScore":30,"summary":"The README is genuinely strong for a micro-library: 4,000+ characters across 11 sections, with runnable usage examples for every supported input shape (strings, objects, arrays, nesting) and a clear explanation of the multiple module formats (ESM/CJS/UMD) and the separate 'lite' mode. Beyond the README, though, there is no further knowledge layer — no /docs, no ADRs, no CHANGELOG.md, no CONTRIBUTING.md, and no generated API docs despite shipping TypeScript type definitions.","evidence":["Substantial README (4002 chars, 11 sections)"],"strengths":["Substantial, example-driven README covering every usage pattern and edge case","Documents all three distribution formats (ESM/CJS/UMD) and the size trade-off of the 'lite' variant","CI/coverage/license badges give at-a-glance project health signals"],"gaps":["No CONTRIBUTING.md guiding external or agentic contributions","No CHANGELOG.md despite a visible versioning cadence in commit history","No /docs directory or architecture decision records","No generated API reference (e.g. typedoc) despite shipping .d.ts type definitions"]},{"id":"D6","name":"Code Quality & Guardrails","weight":0.07,"score":23,"signalScore":24,"llmScore":22,"summary":"A formatter is configured, and the project ships hand-written TypeScript declaration files (clsx.d.ts) plus @ts-check annotations in bin/index.js, giving light type-safety guardrails. But there is no visible linter (ESLint/Biome), no pre-commit hooks (husky/lint-staged), no CODEOWNERS, and — corroborated by the process signals — a 0% PR review coverage rate across 6 recently merged PRs, meaning whatever guardrails exist are not enforced through review.","evidence":["Formatter configured","PR review coverage 0% (6 merged · 100% small · 0% reverted)"],"strengths":["Formatter configured","Hand-maintained TypeScript type definitions (clsx.d.ts) provide a consumer-facing type contract","@ts-check annotations used in bin/index.js for lightweight type checking outside a full tsconfig"],"gaps":["No linter (ESLint/Biome/etc.) detected","No pre-commit hooks (husky/lint-staged/.pre-commit-config)","No CODEOWNERS or PR template found","0% reviewed-merge rate means existing guardrails aren't gated by human or automated review"]},{"id":"D7","name":"Commit & Velocity Signals","weight":0.07,"score":49,"signalScore":50,"llmScore":48,"summary":"Commit history shows active, healthy maintenance cadence with 73% conventional-commit style ('fix(types):', 'feat:', 'chore(ci):') and 100% small-batch PRs merging quickly (median 7.8h to merge, 0.4h to first review). However, 0% of PRs in the sample show an approving review and there is no trace of AI co-authorship (no Co-Authored-By: Claude/Copilot trailers, 0% AI-involved rate), so velocity here reflects a fast solo-maintainer rhythm rather than an AI-augmented or team-reviewed one.","evidence":["30 recent commits analyzed","Conventional commit style (73%)","Active commit cadence"],"strengths":["Active, consistent commit cadence (30 recent commits analyzed)","73% conventional-commit-style messages","100% small-batch PRs with fast median time-to-merge (7.8h) and time-to-first-review (0.4h)"],"gaps":["0% AI-involved commit/PR rate — no co-author trailers or bot commits detected","0% reviewed-merge rate despite the fast merge cadence — speed isn't paired with review discipline","27% of commits fall outside conventional-commit format"]},{"id":"D8","name":"AI Process & Harness","weight":0.12,"score":2,"signalScore":0,"llmScore":3,"summary":"There is no dedicated AI process or harness anywhere in this repository: no evals or golden-output tests for LLM behavior, no prompt or agent-spec library, no agent-readable runbooks, and no structured Definition-of-Done or CONTRIBUTING guidance aimed at agent contributors. Nothing here suggests AI is used through any repeatable, verified process — the maintainer's clearly disciplined manual workflow simply hasn't extended into AI-specific harnessing yet.","evidence":["No dedicated AI process/harness detected (e.g. evals, prompt library, agent runbooks)"],"strengths":[],"gaps":["No evals/golden-test directory for AI-generated output","No prompt or agent-spec library (prompts/, .claude/agents/)","No agent-oriented operational docs or runbooks","No structured Definition-of-Done or agent-facing contribution process"]},{"id":"D9","name":"Supply Chain & Security","weight":0.09,"score":18,"signalScore":18,"llmScore":18,"summary":"Supply-chain and security posture is weak across nearly every control graded. The default branch `master` is unprotected (no required PR, reviews, or status checks), the sole workflow sets no explicit `permissions:` scope, none of the 3 Action references are pinned by SHA, and there is no SAST, dependency-update automation, SECURITY.md, release signing/provenance, or SBOM generation anywhere in the pipeline. The only passing control is the absence of dangerous workflow patterns (script 